{
  "title": "7 ADR traps that cause stale decisions",
  "content": "There's a gap between how regulatory compliance is taught and how it actually behaves under load. The tutorials all show the happy path. Here's what a colleague hitting this for the first time needs to know.\n\nArchitecture decision records (ADRs) are supposed to be the memory of a codebase. Yet many teams write them once and never look back, leading to decisions that are forgotten, contradicted, or silently reversed. The problem isn't that ADRs are hard to write — it's that they're easy to write badly and even easier to let rot. This post covers seven specific traps that turn ADRs from a useful tool into a documentation graveyard, each with a concrete fix. By the end, you'll know how to avoid the most common failure modes and keep your decision log alive.\n\n## 1. The template trap: using a heavyweight format that nobody fills in\n\n**What it does:** Provides a structured template for capturing decisions, often with sections like Context, Decision, Status, Consequences, and Alternatives Considered. The most well-known is Michael Nygard's original ADR format, which is intentionally minimal. But some teams adopt elaborate templates with dozens of fields, hoping to capture every nuance.\n\n**Concrete strength:** A well-designed template ensures consistency and makes it easy to compare decisions across time. When done right, it forces the author to articulate the problem and the trade-offs, which is half the battle.\n\n**Concrete weakness:** Overly detailed templates become a barrier. Authors procrastinate because filling in 15 sections feels like writing a design doc. The result is that decisions go undocumented, or the record is filled with boilerplate that adds no value. The ADR becomes a bureaucratic exercise rather than a communication tool.\n\n**When it applies:** Teams that are new to ADRs and need guardrails. A minimal template (title, status, context, decision, consequences) is usually enough.\n\n**When it fails:** When the template requires information that isn't known at decision time, or when it duplicates content from other documents (e.g., a full design doc). The ADR should be a lightweight index, not the whole story.\n\n**Best for:** Teams that want to start small and iterate. Begin with Nygard's format and only add sections if you find yourself repeatedly needing them.\n\n## 2. The status trap: leaving decisions in \"Proposed\" forever\n\n**What it does:** Tracks the lifecycle of a decision via a status field: proposed, accepted, rejected, deprecated, superseded. This is a core part of the ADR concept — it tells readers whether the decision is still in effect.\n\n**Concrete strength:** Status makes it immediately clear which decisions are binding and which are historical. It also provides a natural way to handle reversals: you don't delete the old ADR; you mark it as superseded by a new one.\n\n**Concrete weakness:** Teams often forget to update the status. An ADR might be marked \"proposed\" long after the decision was made, or \"accepted\" even though it was later reversed. Without discipline, the status field becomes misleading.\n\n**When it applies:** Every ADR should have a status. It's the single most important piece of metadata.\n\n**When it fails:** When there's no process to review and update statuses. If nobody owns the ADR log, statuses drift.\n\n**Best for:** Teams that institute a lightweight review cadence (e.g., quarterly) to audit ADR statuses and mark superseded ones. A simple script that lists all ADRs with status \"proposed\" older than 30 days can prompt action.\n\n## 3. The orphan trap: storing ADRs where nobody looks\n\n**What it does:** ADRs need a home. Common choices: a `/docs/adr` directory in the repo, a wiki, a dedicated tool like ADR Tools, or a project management system.\n\n**Concrete strength:** Keeping ADRs in the repo alongside code means they're versioned with the code they describe. Developers can find them when browsing the codebase, and pull requests can include ADR updates.\n\n**Concrete weakness:** If ADRs live in a separate wiki or tool, they quickly become orphaned. Developers forget they exist, and the wiki becomes a graveyard. Even in-repo ADRs can be overlooked if the directory isn't linked from the README.\n\n**When it applies:** In-repo is generally best for engineering decisions that affect the codebase. For cross-team or organizational decisions, a shared location might be needed, but it should still be linked from relevant repos.\n\n**When it fails:** When the location is not discoverable. If a new developer joins and can't find the ADR log within five minutes, it's effectively lost.\n\n**Best for:** Teams that use a monorepo or have a strong documentation culture. Start with `/docs/adr` and add a prominent link in the README. Consider using a tool like `adr-tools` (a command-line tool) to manage the directory structure and numbering.\n\n## 4. The numbering trap: inconsistent IDs that break references\n\n**What it does:** ADRs are typically numbered sequentially (0001, 0002, etc.) for easy reference. This numbering is often used in commit messages, code comments, and other ADRs (e.g., \"Supersedes ADR-0005\").\n\n**Concrete strength:** Sequential numbers provide a stable, human-readable identifier. They make it easy to refer to a decision in conversation or in code.\n\n**Concrete weakness:** If numbers are assigned ad-hoc (e.g., by different people at different times), collisions or gaps occur. Worse, if an ADR is deleted or renumbered, references break. Some teams use dates or UUIDs, which are unique but less human-friendly.\n\n**When it applies:** Any team that references ADRs in other artifacts. Consistency matters more than the scheme itself.\n\n**When it fails:** When there's no single source of truth for the next number. Without tooling, manual numbering leads to mistakes.\n\n**Best for:** Teams that use a tool to generate ADRs. `adr-tools` automatically assigns the next number. If you're doing it manually, establish a convention (e.g., use the next integer based on the highest existing number) and enforce it in code review.\n\n## 5. The context trap: writing decisions without the problem\n\n**What it does:** The Context section explains the forces at play: the problem, constraints, and alternatives considered. It's what makes an ADR useful to someone who wasn't in the room.\n\n**Concrete strength:** A good context section prevents future readers from re-litigating a decision without understanding why it was made. It also helps new team members come up to speed.\n\n**Concrete weakness:** Authors often skip context, assuming it's obvious. They write \"We will use PostgreSQL\" without explaining what problem that solves or what alternatives were rejected. Months later, nobody remembers why.\n\n**When it applies:** Every ADR. Even seemingly trivial decisions benefit from a sentence or two of context.\n\n**When it fails:** When the context is too vague (\"We need a database\") or too detailed (copying the entire design doc). The sweet spot is a few paragraphs that capture the essential trade-offs.\n\n**Best for:** Teams that review ADRs in pull requests. A reviewer can ask, \"What was the alternative?\" to prompt better context. A simple checklist item: \"Does the Context section explain the problem and at least one alternative?\"\n\n## 6. The workflow trap: no integration with the development process\n\n**What it does:** ADRs are most effective when they're part of the workflow: created when a significant decision is made, reviewed alongside code, and referenced in onboarding.\n\n**Concrete strength:** Integrating ADRs into pull requests ensures they're written at the right time and reviewed by peers. Linking to ADRs from code comments helps future maintainers.\n\n**Concrete weakness:** Without integration, ADRs become an afterthought. Teams either write them too late (after the decision is implemented) or not at all. There's no trigger to create one.\n\n**When it applies:** Any team that wants ADRs to be more than a compliance exercise.\n\n**When it fails:** When the process is too heavy. Requiring an ADR for every minor decision leads to fatigue. Define what constitutes a \"significant\" decision (e.g., choosing a framework, changing a data model, adopting a new service).\n\n**Best for:** Teams that use pull requests. Add a checklist item: \"Does this PR require an ADR?\" and link to the ADR directory. For decisions made outside code (e.g., in meetings), designate a scribe to create the ADR within a day.\n\n## 7. The staleness trap: never revisiting or superseding decisions\n\n**What it does:** ADRs are meant to be immutable: once accepted, you don't edit them. Instead, you supersede them with a new ADR when the decision changes. This preserves the history of why things are the way they are.\n\n**Concrete strength:** Immutability provides an audit trail. You can see how thinking evolved over time.\n\n**Concrete weakness:** Teams often forget to supersede. An ADR might remain \"accepted\" even though the team has moved on. The result is a log that contradicts reality, eroding trust in the entire system.\n\n**When it applies:** Whenever a decision is reversed or significantly modified.\n\n**When it fails:** When there's no trigger to revisit. Without a periodic review, stale ADRs accumulate.\n\n**Best for:** Teams that schedule a quarterly ADR review. During the review, check each \"accepted\" ADR: is it still true? If not, create a new ADR that supersedes it and update the old one's status. A simple script can list all ADRs and their last-modified dates to spot ones that haven't been touched in a year.\n\n## How to prioritise which traps to fix first\n\nNot all traps are equally damaging. If you're just starting, focus on the ones that cause the most immediate pain:\n\n1. **Orphan trap (discoverability)** — If nobody can find your ADRs, nothing else matters. Fix this first by putting them in the repo and linking from the README.\n2. **Status trap** — Without accurate statuses, readers can't trust the log. Implement a lightweight review to update statuses.\n3. **Context trap** — Poor context makes ADRs useless for future readers. Enforce a minimal context section in code review.\n4. **Workflow trap** — Integrate ADR creation into pull requests to ensure they're written at the right time.\n5. **Staleness trap** — Schedule a quarterly review to supersede outdated decisions.\n6. **Template trap** — Start with a minimal template; expand only if needed.\n7. **Numbering trap** — Use a tool to avoid collisions; it's a minor issue compared to the others.\n\nThis order reflects the impact on day-to-day usability. A discoverable, up-to-date log with minimal context is far more valuable than a perfectly formatted but stale one.\n\n## A minimal ADR template you can adopt today\n\nIf you're starting from scratch, use this lightweight template (based on Nygard's format):\n\n```markdown\n# ADR-0001: Use PostgreSQL for the primary datastore\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nWe need a relational database for the order management service. The team has experience with PostgreSQL, and it supports the required ACID guarantees. Alternatives considered: MySQL (less familiar to the team), MongoDB (not relational).\n\n## Decision\n\nWe will use PostgreSQL 14 as the primary datastore for the order service.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Positive: Team familiarity reduces onboarding time; strong community support.\n- Negative: Requires managing a stateful service; backups and replication need to be configured.\n- Neutral: We'll need to choose an ORM or query builder.\n```\n\nThis template is intentionally short. It captures the essentials without overwhelming the author. You can add sections like \"Alternatives\" or \"References\" if needed, but start simple.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**What is an architecture decision record?**\n\nAn architecture decision record (ADR) is a short text file that captures a significant decision made about a system's architecture, including the context, the decision itself, and its consequences. It's stored alongside the code and versioned with it, providing a historical record of why things are the way they are. ADRs are meant to be immutable: when a decision changes, you write a new ADR that supersedes the old one.\n\n**How do I convince my team to write ADRs?**\n\nStart small: write one ADR for a recent decision and share it. Show how it answers questions that would otherwise require digging through chat history. Integrate ADR creation into pull requests with a simple checklist item. Avoid mandating ADRs for every decision; focus on the significant ones. Over time, the value becomes obvious as new team members use the log to ramp up.\n\n**Where should I store ADRs?**\n\nThe most common and effective place is in the code repository, typically under `/docs/adr` or `/doc/adr`. This keeps them versioned with the code and easily discoverable. Link to the directory from the README. For cross-team decisions, you might also publish them to a shared wiki, but the repo should be the source of truth. Avoid storing them only in a separate tool that developers don't visit regularly.\n\n**How often should ADRs be reviewed?**\n\nA quarterly review is a good cadence for most teams. During the review, check each ADR with status \"accepted\" to see if it's still accurate. If a decision has changed, create a new ADR to supersede it and update the old one's status to \"superseded.\" Also look for ADRs stuck in \"proposed\" for too long. The goal is to keep the log trustworthy, not to create busywork.\n\n## Your next step: audit your ADR directory in 30 minutes\n\nOpen your terminal and run this command from the root of your repository (assuming ADRs are in `docs/adr`):\n\n```bash\nfind docs/adr -name '*.md' -exec grep -l 'Status: Proposed' {} \\;\n```\n\nThis lists all ADRs still marked as proposed. For each one, decide: is it still relevant? If yes, move it to accepted (or discuss it). If no, mark it as rejected or superseded. This simple audit takes half an hour and immediately improves the accuracy of your decision log. If you don't have an ADR directory yet, create one and write your first ADR for the most recent significant decision your team made.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [PostgreSQL documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
